  <p>[ <a class="reference internal" href="../index.html#cli-aws"><span class="std std-ref">aws</span></a> . <a class="reference internal" href="index.html#cli-aws-ec2"><span class="std std-ref">ec2</span></a> ]</p>
<div class="section" id="modify-volume">
<span id="cli-aws-ec2-modify-volume"></span><h1>modify-volume<a class="headerlink" href="#modify-volume" title="Permalink to this heading">¶</a></h1>
<div class="section" id="description">
<h2>Description<a class="headerlink" href="#description" title="Permalink to this heading">¶</a></h2>
<p>You can modify several parameters of an existing EBS volume, including volume size, volume type, and IOPS capacity. If your EBS volume is attached to a current-generation EC2 instance type, you might be able to apply these changes without stopping the instance or detaching the volume from it. For more information about modifying EBS volumes, see <a class="reference external" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/ebs-modify-volume.html">Amazon EBS Elastic Volumes</a> in the <em>Amazon EBS User Guide</em> .</p>
<p>When you complete a resize operation on your volume, you need to extend the volume’s file-system size to take advantage of the new storage capacity. For more information, see <a class="reference external" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/recognize-expanded-volume-linux.html">Extend the file system</a> .</p>
<p>For more information, see <a class="reference external" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/ebs/latest/userguide/monitoring-volume-modifications.html">Monitor the progress of volume modifications</a> in the <em>Amazon EBS User Guide</em> .</p>
<p>With previous-generation instance types, resizing an EBS volume might require detaching and reattaching the volume or stopping and restarting the instance.</p>
<p>After you initiate a volume modification, you must wait for that modification to reach the <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">completed</span></code> state before you can initiate another modification for the same volume. You can modify a volume up to four times within a rolling 24-hour period, as long as the volume is in the <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">in-use</span></code> or <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">available</span></code> state, and all previous modifications for that volume are <code class="docutils literal notranslate"><span class="pre">completed</span></code> . If you exceed this limit, you get an error message that indicates when you can perform your next modification.</p>
<p>See also: <a class="reference external" href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/goto/WebAPI/ec2-2016-11-15/ModifyVolume">AWS API Documentation</a></p>
</div>
<div class="section" id="synopsis">
<h2>Synopsis<a class="headerlink" href="#synopsis" title="Permalink to this heading">¶</a></h2>
